1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,087
Every plane that has ever crashed left one survivor. It is not a person.

2
00:00:06,233 --> 00:00:08,650
That survivor is me. I am the flight recorder.

3
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:14,827
I sit in the tail, I listen to everything, and I am built to walk out of the one moment nobody else does.

4
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:19,414
People call me a black box. I have never been black.

5
00:00:19,414 --> 00:00:21,922
I am, and always have been, bright orange.

6
00:00:23,066 --> 00:00:29,077
I can survive being slammed into the ground at three thousand four hundred times the force of gravity.

7
00:00:29,077 --> 00:00:32,083
Then set on fire at eleven hundred degrees Celsius.

8
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:35,422
Then sunk under three kilometres of ocean for two years.

9
00:00:36,566 --> 00:00:44,194
And when investigators finally open me up, the tiny board inside will tell them exactly how hundreds of people died — down to the last second.

10
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:48,579
So what is actually inside me? Who built the first one?

11
00:00:48,579 --> 00:00:53,300
And why can a fist-sized box survive a crash that a two hundred tonne aircraft cannot?

12
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:57,907
This is the story of the machine that outlives the disaster.

13
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:01,149
What's actually inside a black box.

14
00:01:02,266 --> 00:01:05,035
Chapter one. The man they told to stop.

15
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:13,750
In nineteen thirty-four, a de Havilland airliner called the Miss Hobart fell into Bass Strait, off the coast of Australia.

16
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:18,679
Eleven people died. The wreck was never found. The cause was never determined.

17
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:22,769
On board was a minister named Hubert Warren.

18
00:01:22,769 --> 00:01:28,707
He left behind a nine-year-old son, and one last gift — a small crystal radio set.

19
00:01:29,833 --> 00:01:33,449
That boy, David Warren, grew up to become a fuel scientist.

20
00:01:33,449 --> 00:01:43,312
And decades later he sat on the panel investigating the world's first jetliner, the de Havilland Comet, as it fell out of the sky again and again with no explanation.

21
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:46,513
Warren had a simple, radical thought.

22
00:01:46,513 --> 00:01:52,061
What if the cockpit could remember its own final minutes? What if the plane could testify?

23
00:01:53,199 --> 00:01:57,754
By nineteen fifty-six he had built it — the A R L Flight Memory Unit.

24
00:01:57,754 --> 00:02:02,916
It stored four hours of cockpit voices and instrument readings on a loop of fine steel wire.

25
00:02:04,066 --> 00:02:04,785
Australia's response?

26
00:02:04,785 --> 00:02:09,095
The air force said it would yield, quote, more expletives than explanations.

27
00:02:09,095 --> 00:02:13,764
Civil aviation said it had little immediate use. Pilots feared being spied on.

28
00:02:13,764 --> 00:02:16,997
He was told to get back to fuel research.

29
00:02:18,133 --> 00:02:21,396
It took a visitor from Britain to see it.

30
00:02:21,396 --> 00:02:28,647
In nineteen fifty-eight a British official watched a demonstration, understood instantly, and flew Warren to London to show it off.

31
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:30,097
The idea finally caught.

32
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:36,901
Ironically, Australia became the first country on Earth to make cockpit voice recorders mandatory, in nineteen sixty.

33
00:02:36,901 --> 00:02:41,901
The United States followed with data recorders, then voice recorders through the mid nineteen sixties.

34
00:02:41,901 --> 00:02:43,902
The witness was now the law.

35
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:54,768
He lost his father to a crash with no answers, and spent his life making sure no family ever had to hear those words again — cause, unknown.

36
00:02:54,768 --> 00:02:59,635
When David Warren died, his coffin was labelled: flight recorder inventor. Do not open.

37
00:03:00,766 --> 00:03:03,613
Chapter two. Why orange is called black.

38
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:08,113
Here is the first myth, and it's about my own name.

39
00:03:08,113 --> 00:03:15,182
I am painted in a colour officially called international orange, so searchers can spot me in wreckage, in mud, on the sea floor.

40
00:03:16,299 --> 00:03:21,155
So why black box? Nobody can prove it. The best-documented answer is the least dramatic.

41
00:03:21,155 --> 00:03:26,334
In World War Two, British crews called the secret electronic units in their aircraft black boxes.

42
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:32,808
The phrase was already slang for a mysterious device you judge only by what goes in and what comes out.

43
00:03:33,933 --> 00:03:38,368
You'll hear it was named because fire turns it black. That one is folklore.

44
00:03:38,368 --> 00:03:42,487
The name existed long before anyone filmed a charred recorder. Orange came first.

45
00:03:42,487 --> 00:03:46,289
Black was just what engineers called a box they couldn't see inside.

46
00:03:47,433 --> 00:03:49,392
Chapter three. Open me up.

47
00:03:50,533 --> 00:03:54,033
Now the part you came for. What is actually inside.

48
00:03:54,033 --> 00:03:57,534
And the surprise is how little of me actually matters.

49
00:03:58,666 --> 00:04:02,784
Most of my body is just electronics that do the recording in flight.

50
00:04:02,784 --> 00:04:08,485
In a crash, all of that is allowed to die. Only one part has to survive — this.

51
00:04:08,485 --> 00:04:11,335
The crash survivable memory unit. Everything else is packaging.

52
00:04:12,466 --> 00:04:17,294
The outer wall is stainless steel or titanium, about a quarter of an inch thick.

53
00:04:17,294 --> 00:04:20,512
That's the skin that takes the impact and the crush.

54
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:25,929
Inside that is a block of dry silica thermal insulation — a full inch of it.

55
00:04:25,929 --> 00:04:33,179
That's the layer that lets me sit in an eleven hundred degree fire for an hour while the memory in the middle stays cool enough to survive.

56
00:04:34,299 --> 00:04:39,581
And at the very centre, wrapped in all that armour, is the only thing that actually matters.

57
00:04:39,581 --> 00:04:43,931
A small stack of solid-state memory chips. No moving parts. Smaller than your hand.

58
00:04:43,931 --> 00:04:48,901
That is the real black box. The rest is a suit of armour built around it.

59
00:04:50,033 --> 00:04:51,773
How tough is that armour, exactly?

60
00:04:51,773 --> 00:04:55,832
These are the certified numbers I have to pass before I'm allowed to fly.

61
00:04:56,966 --> 00:04:57,280
Impact.

62
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:04,508
Fired into a target at three thousand four hundred times the force of gravity, for six and a half thousandths of a second.

63
00:05:04,508 --> 00:05:07,964
That's like hitting a wall at hundreds of miles an hour.

64
00:05:09,099 --> 00:05:11,944
Fire. Eleven hundred degrees Celsius for a full hour.

65
00:05:11,944 --> 00:05:18,581
Then a slow burn — two hundred and sixty degrees for ten hours — in case I'm buried in smouldering wreckage.

66
00:05:19,699 --> 00:05:23,986
Crush. Five thousand pounds of static force on every axis. Puncture.

67
00:05:23,986 --> 00:05:29,831
A five hundred pound weight dropped onto a steel spike aimed straight at my heart.

68
00:05:29,831 --> 00:05:33,728
Pressure. Held at the equivalent of twenty thousand feet underwater.

69
00:05:34,866 --> 00:05:43,333
And then, after all of that, I have to be soaked in seawater for thirty days and still give up every byte.

70
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:46,412
That is what survivable means. Not indestructible. Survivable.

71
00:05:47,533 --> 00:05:49,700
So here's the obvious question everyone asks.

72
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:54,654
If this little box survives anything — why not build the whole plane out of it?

73
00:05:54,654 --> 00:05:58,060
Hold that thought. It's coming up, and the answer is beautiful.

74
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,551
Chapter four. What I actually hear.

75
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:07,924
There are really two of me on every airliner. The first is the cockpit voice recorder.

76
00:06:07,924 --> 00:06:08,578
It listens.

77
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:18,874
A microphone in the cockpit ceiling picks up everything — the pilots' words, the switches, the alarms, the engines, even the tone of a voice starting to break.

78
00:06:18,874 --> 00:06:21,168
Plus a channel for each crew member.

79
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:26,017
Older recorders kept only the last thirty minutes. Then two hours.

80
00:06:26,017 --> 00:06:32,437
New aircraft in Europe now keep a full twenty-five hours, and the United States is finally adopting the same.

81
00:06:32,437 --> 00:06:38,182
It runs on a loop, always erasing the oldest audio — which matters more than you'd think.

82
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:43,662
The second recorder is the flight data recorder. It doesn't listen. It knows.

83
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:47,990
The earliest ones tracked five things. Then eleven.

84
00:06:47,990 --> 00:07:01,153
Today I'm required to record at least eighty-eight parameters — and modern jets log well over a thousand: altitude, airspeed, every control input, every engine reading, many times a second, for twenty-five hours.

85
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:07,085
And here's a detail most people miss. Both of us live back here — in the tail.

86
00:07:07,085 --> 00:07:08,211
Not because it's convenient.

87
00:07:08,211 --> 00:07:14,122
Because in most crashes the tail is the last part to hit, and the furthest from the fuel and the fire.

88
00:07:14,122 --> 00:07:16,093
Where I sit is a survival decision.

89
00:07:17,233 --> 00:07:20,394
Chapter five. How to find a box on the sea floor.

90
00:07:21,533 --> 00:07:27,097
But surviving is useless if nobody can find me. So when I touch water, something wakes up.

91
00:07:28,233 --> 00:07:34,910
A beacon on my side starts to ping — a sound pulse at thirty-seven point five kilohertz, once every second.

92
00:07:34,910 --> 00:07:38,917
It's a heartbeat, calling out from the dark, saying: I'm down here.

93
00:07:40,066 --> 00:07:42,882
For decades that heartbeat lasted just thirty days.

94
00:07:42,882 --> 00:07:46,753
And that number nearly cost the world its most famous mystery.

95
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:54,711
In two thousand fourteen, a Boeing seven seven seven — Malaysia Airlines flight M H three seventy — vanished.

96
00:07:54,711 --> 00:07:59,730
Search teams didn't know where to look until the days were already burning down.

97
00:07:59,730 --> 00:08:04,749
My beacons likely fell silent before anyone arrived. The recorders have never been found.

98
00:08:05,866 --> 00:08:10,977
So the rules changed. Now that beacon has to last ninety days, not thirty.

99
00:08:10,977 --> 00:08:17,182
And large aircraft carry a second, low-frequency beacon that can be heard from far, far further away.

100
00:08:17,182 --> 00:08:19,737
M H three seventy rewrote my design.

101
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:23,295
Chapter six. The two-year recovery.

102
00:08:24,433 --> 00:08:29,280
Let me show you what it looks like when it works. Air France four forty-seven.

103
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:33,805
Twenty oh nine. An Airbus stalled into the Atlantic in the dead of night.

104
00:08:33,805 --> 00:08:35,744
Two hundred and twenty-eight people lost.

105
00:08:36,866 --> 00:08:39,291
My beacons died in thirty days.

106
00:08:39,291 --> 00:08:47,373
It took nearly two years, deep-tow sonar and robot submarines, to find the debris field — thirteen thousand feet down.

107
00:08:47,373 --> 00:08:49,797
Almost four kilometres of black water.

108
00:08:50,933 --> 00:08:55,403
In twenty eleven they lifted the memory modules from the sea bed.

109
00:08:55,403 --> 00:09:00,247
Two years of pressure and salt water — and every byte was intact.

110
00:09:00,247 --> 00:09:05,090
Over a single weekend, investigators read the entire final two hours. The stall.

111
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:06,580
The confusion. The truth.

112
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:10,824
That is the whole point of me. Not to prevent the crash.

113
00:09:10,824 --> 00:09:15,511
To make sure it is never a mystery. To make sure it never happens the same way twice.

114
00:09:16,633 --> 00:09:20,218
But what happens when I come back burned, shattered, or soaked?

115
00:09:20,218 --> 00:09:23,477
That's where the real magic is — in the lab.

116
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,541
A sea-recovered board is never allowed to dry out.

117
00:09:27,541 --> 00:09:30,156
Drying crystallises the salt and eats the circuits.

118
00:09:30,156 --> 00:09:37,348
So technicians keep it wet, then rinse it in pure, clean water to wash the salt away before they dare apply power.

119
00:09:38,466 --> 00:09:44,435
And if my own electronics are destroyed but the memory chips are alive, they use a trick called the golden chassis.

120
00:09:44,435 --> 00:09:52,677
They lift the surviving chips out of the wreck and drop them into a pristine, identical recorder in the lab — and read them as if nothing ever happened.

121
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:56,491
Chapter seven. Everything you think you know.

122
00:09:57,633 --> 00:10:00,927
Time to open the ones you've heard a hundred times, and grade them.

123
00:10:00,927 --> 00:10:02,701
And I get to be the judge.

124
00:10:03,833 --> 00:10:07,930
Myth one. Why not build the whole plane out of black box material?

125
00:10:07,930 --> 00:10:10,767
Here's the beautiful answer. I survive because I'm small.

126
00:10:10,767 --> 00:10:15,179
My armour weighs a few kilos on a two hundred tonne aircraft — nothing.

127
00:10:15,179 --> 00:10:21,168
Scale that armour up to an entire fuselage and the plane is too heavy to ever leave the ground.

128
00:10:21,168 --> 00:10:26,526
A plane has to be light enough to fly. I get to be heavy because I'm tiny.

129
00:10:26,526 --> 00:10:27,787
Verdict: physics says no.

130
00:10:28,933 --> 00:10:34,073
Myth two. Black boxes are indestructible. False — and I'd never claim otherwise.

131
00:10:34,073 --> 00:10:38,818
On September eleventh, the two recorders in the towers were never recovered.

132
00:10:38,818 --> 00:10:45,540
Some crashes exceed even my limits. I am crash survivable. That is a promise, not a boast.

133
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:46,331
Verdict: false.

134
00:10:47,466 --> 00:10:53,667
Myth three. They record video of the cockpit. Today — no. I capture audio and data only.

135
00:10:53,667 --> 00:11:01,326
Investigators have pushed for cockpit cameras for over a decade, and pilots' unions have fought it just as hard, over privacy.

136
00:11:01,326 --> 00:11:06,797
So for now, I listen. I don't watch. Verdict: false, but the fight isn't over.

137
00:11:07,933 --> 00:11:11,898
Myth four. Someone is listening to you the whole flight. No one is.

138
00:11:11,898 --> 00:11:17,694
I record onto a loop that erases itself, and no human ever hears it — unless there's an accident.

139
00:11:17,694 --> 00:11:24,100
In many countries the law forbids that audio from being used to punish a crew, or from ever being made public.

140
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,846
I am a witness, not a spy. Verdict: false.

141
00:11:27,966 --> 00:11:30,552
Chapter eight. The recorder that ejects itself.

142
00:11:31,700 --> 00:11:33,755
So what happens to me next?

143
00:11:33,755 --> 00:11:37,867
Because M H three seventy proved that sometimes, being tough isn't enough.

144
00:11:37,867 --> 00:11:40,608
Sometimes you have to be found — fast.

145
00:11:41,733 --> 00:11:47,636
The first answer already flies on military jets and now on some airliners, like the Airbus A three fifty.

146
00:11:47,636 --> 00:11:48,568
A deployable recorder.

147
00:11:48,568 --> 00:11:56,335
In a crash it's fired clear of the tail — and because it floats, it rides on the surface instead of sinking four kilometres down.

148
00:11:57,466 --> 00:12:04,291
The second answer is to never lose the data at all — stream it live to the ground, a black box in the cloud.

149
00:12:04,291 --> 00:12:09,979
The problem is brutal volume and cost, so for now it's used only in bursts, triggered when something goes wrong.

150
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:15,913
And after M H three seventy, the world built a global tracking rule called GADSS.

151
00:12:15,913 --> 00:12:24,579
An aircraft in distress must now report its position at least once a minute — automatically, with no way for anyone on board to switch it off.

152
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,618
So that's me.

153
00:12:26,618 --> 00:12:33,660
A fist of memory, wrapped in armour, sitting in the tail — waiting for the worst day, so that day is never wasted.

154
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:37,640
I am the promise that the truth survives, even when nothing else does.

155
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:41,008
I am the only witness that walks out of every crash.

156
00:12:42,133 --> 00:12:50,859
If you want to see everything I remember put to the test — watch the deadliest crash in history, where two of us on the ground held the answer.

157
00:12:50,859 --> 00:12:57,780
Or the one flight that beat me: M H three seventy, the Boeing seven seven seven that vanished from every radar on Earth.

158
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:00,109
I'm the black box.

159
00:13:00,109 --> 00:13:05,248
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown — and next time, we'll open the ones that solved the impossible.

